Status: Task 6 complete after profile migration, twelve bounded batches, and a
final relevant-Anvil closure review

Last verified: 2026-07-14 against the live local runtime and repositories

## Original Review Boundary

The initial inventory-only slice approved after Checkpoint C did not move,
delete, shrink, or disable any instruction source. Vik subsequently approved
the collaboration reconciliation and global shrink as separate bounded batches.

Non-goals that still apply:

- no edits to project `AGENTS.md` files;
- no hook or skill changes;
- no native Codex memory changes or disablement (Task 7);
- no skill-journal synthesis (Task 8);
- no routine or repeated Brain judge runs; the one explicitly authorized,
  scoped Codex closeout call has been consumed and must not be retried or
  expanded without new approval;
- no deletion of Continuity history or migration-driven compact-frame cleanup;
  routine phase-change hygiene remains separate.

","source_ref":""},{"id":2,"file":"docs/plans/2026-06-03-mcp-continuity-v0.md","start_byte":0,"end_byte":1022,"start_line":1,"end_line":26,"content_hash":"5c95c30dcf35d26dc832d14f0d9687a95ce70567a2900ad191d8121a5d32f79b","content":"# Spec: MCP Continuity v0

## Status

Implemented

## Objective

Build Anvil's first MCP adapter so Codex can explicitly read and write workspace continuity during a session.

This is not the full Anvil MCP substrate. It is a narrow continuity adapter over the existing TypeScript core. It should make the useful part of continuity available in-session while preserving the core Anvil principle:

> Continuity is not truth. Current user instruction, current files, command output, tests, and live evidence override stale continuity.

Success means Codex can:

- read the current continuity frame and recent event history through MCP
- record durable decisions, questions, next actions, corrections, hypotheses, and phase changes
- promote specific recorded events into the compact current frame
- avoid arbitrary self-rewriting of continuity state

## Assumptions

- Codex is the first target runtime.
- MCP is for explicit in-session tool calls.
- The Codex `SessionStart` hook remains responsible for startup injection.
